aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.3 have "diff_class_harder ∧ O discriminate → O ¬give_up"; aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "in the case of those who are in different classes the discrimination is a matter of more trouble" and "it ought to be that we discriminate what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy" are true, which the second allows
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.2 with no modality; aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.3 as what ought to be or is permitted: its "should" or "must" does not say which it means [in 1 of 2 runs]
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "in the case of those who are in different classes the discrimination is a matter of more trouble", "it ought to be that we discriminate what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy" and "it ought to be that we discriminate what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy" are true and "it ought to be that it is not so that we give up the attempt to discriminate" and "it ought to be that it is not so that we give up the attempt to discriminate" are false. Is S3, "This, however, should not be a reason for giving up the attempt, but we must observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so.", true then?
aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1 has "O discriminate → O observe_practicable"; aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.2 has "O(discriminate → ¬give_up)" and "O(discriminate → observe_practicable)"; they differ when only "it ought to be that (if we discriminate what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy, then we observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so)", "it ought to be that we discriminate what belongs to each in respect of nearness of connection, goodness or intimacy" and "it ought to be that we observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so" are true, which the first allows; aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.3 has "O discriminate ∧ O ¬give_up → O observe_practicable" [in 1 of 2 runs]
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1 has "O ¬give_up ∧ O observe_practicable"; aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1.3 have "O observe_practicable"; they differ when only "it ought to be that we observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so" and "it ought to be that we observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so" are true, which the second allows
aristotle-ethics-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps aristotle-ethics-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "it ought to be that we observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so", "it ought to be that we observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so" and "it ought to be that we observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so" are true and "it ought to be that it is not so that we give up the attempt to discriminate" is false. Is the conclusion, "This, however, should not be a reason for giving up the attempt, but we must observe the distinctions so far as it is practicable to do so.", true then?
